;; Seon's config: every limit, bound and default of the platform, one key each
;; (owner, 2026-10-21: "the only things that should stay are documented config schemas that we
;; are porting from seon if they are relevant"). v1's shape (~/src/seon config/default.edn):
;; beside each key, its unit, its provenance (an owner ruling, a measurement, the worktree session that set
;; it) and what happens at its bound.
;;
;; - Read once with aero when seon.config loads (root's boot; agents and the fork server are
;;   forked from root and read no environment). Validated against :seon.config/config
;;   (resources/seon/schemas/seon.config*.edn): an unknown key, an invalid value or a missing
;;   required key refuses boot with :seon.config/refused, naming each key.
;; - Root writes the resolved config as facts on main's root entity (platform records, never
;;   carried by a merge): `(seon.config/from-db db)` reads it, on main or any branch.
;; - Overrides come only through #env below, and only where a script or a check sets one, so
;;   this file is the one list of what can be set. A value resolving to nil (an unset #env with
;;   no fallback) is absent, which only an optional key allows.
;; - #profile selects by SEON_PROFILE (default :default); :deepseek runs agents on DeepSeek.
;; - Machine-dependent values are derived at load, never shipped: #seon/checkout "p" is p under
;;   the checkout this process runs from (SEON_CHECKOUT, else JOLT_PWD).
;; - Layers (owner, 2026-10-21: "hand out different edn config files ... or do command line
;;   overrides"): this file, then the checkout's own config/local.edn (gitignored; bin/ports
;;   writes its ports), then the command line's --config FILE and --set :ns/key=edn. Later
;;   layers win key by key; the merged map is validated once; seon.config/sources (and the
;;   :seon.config/sources fact on main) names the layer each key came from.
{;; ==== root and its procs ====================================================================

 ;; Unit: live agents. Root holds a socket (two descriptors) per agent and raises its open-file
 ;; limit to 256 + 2 x this at boot. Provenance: proto/flow f2-final's PROTO_MAX_AGENTS (the
 ;; first version forked a thousand agents at ~1.4 MB each, AGENTS.md).
 ;; At the bound: a spawn is refused (:seon.root/refused "too many live agents").
 :seon.config.root/max-agents 1024

 ;; Unit: writer requests from the api in flight. Provenance: f2-final. At the bound the api
 ;; stops reading its server (Flow's input filter) and reads only answers, so callers are
 ;; pushed back and the writer's answers always have room: the api <-> writer cycle cannot wedge.
 :seon.config.root/api-writes 512
 ;; Unit: messages one api step takes before it applies its graph change (spawns and ends
 ;; become one update!). Provenance: f2-final. At the bound: the step ends and the rest wait.
 :seon.config.root/api-batch 256
 ;; Unit: requests one writer step drains and commits together (group commit). Provenance:
 ;; f2-final. At the bound: the next step takes the rest.
 :seon.config.root/writer-batch 256
 ;; Unit: requests. The writer's in-port. Each agent has at most one write in flight, so a
 ;; buffer the size of the live agents never fills from agents. At the bound senders park.
 :seon.config.root/writer-buf #ref [:seon.config.root/max-agents]
 ;; Unit: on/off. Whether a load of the wiki takes, for a section with no record of the code it was written
 ;; against (:seon.section/read), that record from git: the form ids of the vars it cites as of the commit
 ;; that last changed it, so a section already stale shows as such (docs/health.md#a-stale-section). On
 ;; Seon's wiki that is one git blame per page and one git show per (commit, file), 4 s measured
 ;; 2026-10-23 (303 sections, 139 citing code), once per store. Off (bin/test and bin/check set
 ;; SEON_WIKI_BASELINE=false: every test root has a store of its own): such a section is recorded as of today.
 :seon.config.root/wiki-baseline #boolean #or [#env SEON_WIKI_BASELINE true]
 ;; Unit: main's commits queued for the reconciler. It reads every commit's datoms (an index
 ;; kept by observing them), so the buffer blocks rather than drops: at the bound the writer's
 ;; send parks until the reconciler catches up (a pass costs what the commit changed).
 :seon.config.root/reconciler-buf 1024
 ;; Unit: main commits. The fork server forks a fresh standby after this many, bounding the
 ;; log of commits a promoted standby is sent. Provenance: f2-final.
 :seon.config.root/standby-every 256
 ;; Unit: threads, Chez's parallel-collector sweepers in root, fixed at boot right after the
 ;; fork server is forked (seon.lib/gc-sweepers!). 0: root collects on the thread that triggers
 ;; it. Provenance: session THREADS, 2026-10-21: unfixed, Chez kept one per thread a collection
 ;; found waiting, so root's count drifted with load (0, 5, 15 across identical runs); A/B at
 ;; 0/2/4/8 gave the same GC real time, GC CPU rising with n, threads 37/39/41/45. At the bound: none.
 :seon.config.root/gc-sweepers 0

 ;; Where kept runs live (owner, 2026-10-21: runs are kept, "a perfect record frozen in time").
 ;; bin/root exports SEON_RUNS (<this tree>/runs).
 :seon.config.root/runs-dir #or [#env SEON_RUNS #seon/checkout "runs"]
 ;; The one store (owner, 2026-10-21: one store, many branches, as in production): created
 ;; when missing; every root works on its own main, a branch of the store's seed. bin/check
 ;; sets SEON_STORE to its run's store.
 :seon.config.root/store-path #or [#env SEON_STORE #join [#ref [:seon.config.root/runs-dir] "/store"]]
 ;; Root's main, as a branch name: one main per store, resumed at every boot (owner,
 ;; 2026-10-22: "why are we not resuming one main by default"); a store without it makes it
 ;; from the seed (:db). A run's record is main's commit history. bin/check names each step's
 ;; own main (SEON_MAIN=check-<step>, deleted when the step is green); SEON_MAIN=db is :db itself.
 ;; Provenance: notes/PLAN-branches.md point 1. At the bound: n/a.
 :seon.config.root/main #or [#env SEON_MAIN "main"]
 ;; Root's system declaration (owner, 2026-10-20: the definition lives in a file).
 :seon.config.root/system-path #seon/checkout "resources/seon/system.edn"
 ;; The task files (resources/seon/tasks/<owning-ns>.edn, AGENTS.md "How work is handed out").
 :seon.config.root/tasks-dir #seon/checkout "resources/seon/tasks"

 ;; ==== the store ==============================================================================

 ;; The durable store's konserve backend: LMDB (one env sync per commit) or the POSIX filestore.
 :seon.config.store/backend :lmdb
 ;; Unit: bytes. LMDB's map size, the store's largest size. At the bound LMDB refuses the
 ;; write (MDB_MAP_FULL, a :seon.writer/refused answer). Provenance: the store's first
 ;; default was 1 GiB (seon.db's SEON_LMDB_MB); on 2026-10-21 the standing root's kept store
 ;; reached 747 MB and two agents died on Datahike's fatal-commit-error while recording. The map
 ;; is address space, not memory or disk: without MDB_WRITEMAP (the flags are MDB_NOSYNC) the
 ;; file grows only as it is used, so LMDB's guidance is a map as large as the store may ever
 ;; grow. 64 GiB.
 :seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes 68719476736
 ;; Unit: children per index node. A commit rewrites the nodes it touched, so smaller nodes
 ;; mean fewer bytes printed and written per commit. Provenance: dev/dur.clj measured 512 -> 64
 ;; cut a small commit 27 -> 16 ms with fsync, before the node emitter.
 :seon.config.store/branching-factor 64
 ;; Boolean: whether a store is made keeping history: its temporal indexes (d/history, as-of,
 ;; since; a retraction stays a datom) and each transaction's tx-meta, :db/txInstant included
 ;; (Datahike applies tx-meta only with history). Fixed when the store is made (Datahike's
 ;; store-fixed-record-keys): a kept store opens with what it was made with. Provenance: v1's
 ;; ruling 23 (2026-08-03) and Datahike's default; next had it false, hardcoded, until 2026-10-22.
 ;; Cost measured on seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes (docs/storage.md).
 :seon.config.store/keep-history? true
 ;; Unit: bytes, or :never. A datom value at least this large (a string's characters, a
 ;; collection's elements) is stored once under its own content key in Datahike's store, and
 ;; the index nodes hold a reference to it (datahike.index.persistent-set's out-of-line), so a
 ;; commit rewriting the index tail never writes it again; :never keeps every value inline.
 ;; Provenance: measured 2026-10-22 on seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes
 ;; (docs/storage.md#values-out-of-line); v1 kept values up to 4,096 bytes inline (seon.blob).
 :seon.config.store/value-out-of-line-bytes 1024
 ;; Codec: konserve's per-value compressor for every stored value (index nodes, values out of
 ;; line, commits): zstd frames at level 3 through libzstd (konserve.jolt.zstd, built from
 ;; reference-code/zstd), :lz4 frames (liblz4), or :none; a value's header names its codec, so a
 ;; store holding several reads them all. Provenance, 2026-10-22: on 5,006 real eval records
 ;; compressed one by one, zstd-3 2.05x against LZ4 1.49x (replies 2.16x vs 1.56x); on
 ;; seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes, 20 per-turn commits 194 KB vs LZ4's 233 KB at the
 ;; same ~1.9 ms a commit. A trained dictionary (7.3x on the records) waits for a representative
 ;; corpus (owner, 2026-10-22).
 :seon.config.store/compressor :zstd
 ;; Unit: bytes. The node cache of a process (Datahike's :store-cache-bytes): index nodes read
 ;; back from the store, kept by address and shared by every connection and branch db of the
 ;; store in that process (datahike.index.interface/node-cache-key), least recently used out
 ;; first; Jolt's cache counts every node as 4,096 bytes (datahike.index.node-cache), so this
 ;; is 32,768 nodes. At the bound a node is evicted and its next read goes to the store again
 ;; (65-75 us cold against 8.6 us warm per seek). Provenance, measured 2026-10-23
 ;; (notes/global-ids/MEASURED.md §2-3): Datahike's default of 1,000 nodes thrashed on a scan
 ;; of one 115k-datom index (3,704 nodes: a second scan read all again, 270 ms, as cold), and a
 ;; cache holding them scanned in 26 ms and opened a sibling branch reading 8 nodes. 128 MB holds
 ;; about nine such indexes: a thirty-second of :seon.config.machine/memory-mb per process.
 :seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes 134217728
 ;; Boolean: whether this root is its store's only writer process, so it may collect the store
 ;; (seon.db/collect!; docs/storage.md#collection): Datahike's collection sees only its own
 ;; process's in-flight commits (datahike.gc/gc-storage!), so a commit of another process can
 ;; reuse a value the sweep is deleting. False: root never collects, and the store keeps its
 ;; garbage. Provenance, 2026-10-23 (notes/store-gc-bug-2026-10-23.md): the :scripted profile is
 ;; bin/test's and bin/check's, whose roots (a shard's, a step's) share one store; production
 ;; runs one root per store (task seon.db/two-processes-branch-one-store-at-once).
 :seon.config.store/sole-writer? #profile {:default true :scripted false}

 ;; ==== agents =================================================================================

 ;; Unit: MB. The memory Seon itself may use on the machine it targets: a 16 GB MacBook M1
 ;; (owner, 2026-10-22: "run well on a 16GB Macbook M1"), a quarter of it, leaving the rest to
 ;; the OS, the user's work and what agents run (builds, tests). At the bound: seon.check/scale
 ;; fails when root, the fork server and :seon.config.agent/max-running agents would exceed it
 ;; (each agent charged the system's measured free-memory drop per agent).
 :seon.config.machine/memory-mb 4096
 ;; Unit: MB. The most physical footprint one MCP relay (bin/mcp, one per Claude session) may
 ;; reach: stock perl measured 6.3 MB peak (`/usr/bin/time -l`, 2026-10-22) where the Seon binary
 ;; it replaced was 729 MB. At the bound: seon.check/mcp-proof fails, so a relay that boots Seon
 ;; again is red.
 :seon.config.machine/relay-mb 32

 ;; ==== the check harness (seon.check) =========================================================

 ;; Unit: ms a check's Flow ping (seon.check/ping) waits for a proc to take its control message,
 ;; which a proc takes between two steps. Provenance: task seon.check/every-wait-is-the-event-it-means
 ;; (2026-10-22); the pings it replaced waited 1000 and 2000 ms and read a miss as nil or
 ;; :unknown, and a check beside a full bin/test must not mistake a slow step for a missing
 ;; proc. At the bound: the proc is recorded {:blocked pid :ping-ms ..} in the check's report
 ;; and the check that read it fails, naming it.
 :seon.config.check/ping-ms 10000
 ;; Unit: ms over which a check measures that something does not happen: a paused agent's
 ;; request unanswered (lifecycle), a paused proc's flood not taken and a paused writer's
 ;; commits held (backpressure). Provenance: the windows it replaced were 300, 400 and 500 ms.
 ;; Load only makes what it measures likelier. At the bound: the measurement is read.
 :seon.config.check/hold-ms 500
 ;; Unit: ms after scale's agents are killed over which it measures the memory the kernel
 ;; returns (:system-free-returned-mb-per-agent, reported, not checked). Provenance: the
 ;; window it replaced. At the bound: the free memory is read.
 :seon.config.check/pages-ms 500
 ;; Unit: runs running at once (v1's :seon.config.agent/max-running, same value and
 ;; reason): four agents beside the user's work fit a 16 GB machine. A ready task beyond it
 ;; waits for an end; 0 starts nothing new while running work finishes. root's start! may
 ;; pass :max-running for one run; the fact on main says what the run used.
 :seon.config.agent/max-running 4
 ;; Unit: turns a run may take when its task sets none (a task's own
 ;; :seon.config.agent/max-turns wins). At the bound the run ends and the done rule judges it.
 ;; Provenance: v1's live runs measured turns-to-green median 14-40, run 11's p90 32
 ;; (2026-10-18); R4's DeepSeek runs p90 6 with good context. 8 cut ordinary runs below
 ;; v1's median (owner, 2026-10-21: "8 turn cap? wtf? make sensible defaults!"), so the bound
 ;; sits above the measured p90. Tokens are the paid resource; task
 ;; seon.model/budgets-are-data-and-spend-is-measured makes them the budget.
 :seon.config.agent/max-turns 40
 ;; Unit: path, every agent process's working directory, shared, made at the fork server's boot.
 ;; No repository above it: an agent changes code through its REPL, never git (owner, 2026-10-22:
 ;; "no git in agents"); it reads and writes any file by its absolute path. At the bound: n/a.
 :seon.config.agent/cwd "/tmp/seon-agents"
 ;; Unit: messages in each agent channel's buffer (v1's :seon.config.agent/inbox-size 64).
 ;; Small on purpose: a full channel pushes back on its sender (the reader leaves frames in
 ;; the socket).
 :seon.config.agent/inbox-size 64
 ;; Unit: bytes allocated between an agent's collections (Chez's collect-trip-bytes). Chez's
 ;; default scales with the parent heap (E10); a small trip keeps an agent's garbage, and so
 ;; its private pages, small.
 :seon.config.agent/trip-bytes 4194304
 ;; Unit: bytes an agent's heap may grow past what it inherited. Past it an allocation throws
 ;; OutOfMemoryError in the form (an :err item), so a runaway agent cannot take the machine.
 :seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes 1073741824
 ;; Unit: bytes of each unix socket's send and receive buffer (root, the fork server and every
 ;; agent). Provenance: T1, 1 MB makes a 1 MB reply cost what the ring's did. At the bound a
 ;; writer waits for the reader (frames are never dropped).
 :seon.config.agent/sock-buf-bytes 1048576
 ;; Unit: engine ticks (procedure calls and loop back-edges: work done, never time) one form
 ;; an agent's model or an outside agent sends may run (Jolt's run-interruptible tick budget).
 ;; Provenance: session AGENTS, measured on Jolt 2026-10-21: a tight loop ~4e9 ticks/s, an
 ;; allocating one ~7.5e8, so this stops a runaway form after ~5-25 s of work. At the bound
 ;; the form answers the typed :seon.repl/ticks-spent and the request goes on. 0: none.
 :seon.config.agent/form-ticks 20000000000
 ;; Unit: functions. How many of a form's costliest contracted functions (seon.profile/top)
 ;; its reply item and evaluation record keep, each with its exact calls and estimated ms.
 ;; Provenance: worktree session F2's literal 10, made a key (research row top-fns, 2026-10-22). At the
 ;; bound the rest are left out of the item; the record says nothing of them.
 :seon.config.agent/form-top-fns 10
 ;; Unit: engine ticks one proof run (every test of the branch, in its fork) may run. Larger
 ;; than a form's: cost proofs measure large sizes. At the bound the run stops with
 ;; :seon.test/ticks-spent naming the test that was running. 0: none.
 :seon.config.agent/test-ticks 200000000000
 ;; Unit: engine ticks a turn's own test run may do: the task's proofs and the tests the
 ;; branch changed or added (root's ruling (a), 2026-10-22; the reached in-process set is the
 ;; judge's, at the run's end, under test-ticks). Provenance: step E's run 7, where a change
 ;; to seon.problems/report put 294 reached tests in every turn's run and one ran for 2.5 h;
 ;; the same budget as one form's (~5-25 s of work). At the bound the run stops with
 ;; :seon.test/ticks-spent; the tests it did not finish are unknown, never red, and the turn
 ;; says how many and how to run them (my.agent/prove). 0: none.
 :seon.config.agent/turn-test-ticks 20000000000

 ;; ==== merges =================================================================================

 ;; Unit: merge runs at once. They run together only when the definitions they change are
 ;; disjoint (an octopus merge); 1 is git's one-at-a-time merge queue. Provenance: DECISIONS (root's
 ;; calls on R3's questions): octopus merges stay off until one combined test run of all octopus
 ;; members, the par-200 hang and root memory growth with concurrent merges are fixed. The code
 ;; default had become 8 (b7459c3) with nothing writing the fact (GUARDS active bug 1); boot now
 ;; writes this. An octopus merge is turned on by stating more here (or on main's root entity).
 :seon.config.merge/max-running 1
 ;; Unit: conflicts. A merge run whose merge of main has more is refused
 ;; (:too-many-conflicts); a task's own :seon.config.merge/max-conflicts wins.
 :seon.config.merge/max-conflicts 3
 ;; Unit: merge turns without green. Past it the merge run ends (:merge-turns); a task's
 ;; own :seon.config.merge/max-merge-turns wins.
 :seon.config.merge/max-merge-turns 2


 ;; ==== Seon's own source ======================================================================

 ;; Unit: path. The repository whose source is the program (session LOOP, resources/seon/tasks/
 ;; seon.source.edn): main imports its dirs at boot when it holds none of them, and each merge
 ;; that changes them is written to their files and committed on :seon.config.source/branch.
 ;; Default: SEON_SOURCE_ROOT, which bin/root sets to its own checkout (owner, 2026-10-22: no
 ;; store, build it from source; a store, resume it; the command line overrides), so a root started
 ;; with bin/root builds main from its checkout's source. Unset (bin/test, bin/check, a root a test
 ;; boots): absent, no source is imported and nothing is written. config/local.edn or --set names
 ;; another tree: the main tree's names its merges worktree (bin/worktree --path merges), so
 ;; write-back never edits main's files.
 :seon.config.source/root #env SEON_SOURCE_ROOT
 ;; Unit: path. The checkout the merge gate runs a merge's deferred process tests on
 ;; (seon.fx/run-tests: a worktree of it at the merges head, the candidate's files over it,
 ;; prove's entry there). Provenance: root's ruling (a), 2026-10-23, from step E's dry run (a test
 ;; root importing test/ could not host a checkout, and its gated merges were all refused).
 ;; Absent (as here; the standing root's config/local.edn names its merges tree): a merge that
 ;; reaches process tests merges with them recorded unrun on its run, an open problem
 ;; ("merged with N process tests unrun") until they run; root's git merge runs them (bin/prove).
 :seon.config.source/gate-checkout #env SEON_GATE_CHECKOUT
 ;; Unit: paths under the root, read in this order. Their .clj files are namespaces; the .edn
 ;; files under resources/seon/schemas are schema resources (schemas are program: an agent
 ;; changes one with my.schema/register!, and the merge writes it back; owner 2026-10-21).
 ;; test/ is in: a task's proofs and their fixtures live there, and an agent holds main's
 ;; tests only when main imported them (step E run #11, 2026-10-23: a worktree root without
 ;; test/ forked a design whose proof namespace did not exist, 27 turns of no progress). Only
 ;; a root with a source root imports (bin/root's: SEON_SOURCE_ROOT); a test's root states its
 ;; own dirs. It costs each boot the test forms' evaluation (+5 s a restart, measured
 ;; 2026-10-22; task seon.source/test-namespaces-built-into-the-binary).
 :seon.config.source/dirs ["src" "test" "resources/seon/schemas"]
 ;; Unit: qualified var names. A test whose reach (seon.program/reach-index, the compiler's
 ;; facts) references one of these reaches a process of its own: a shell, a fork, a root booted
 ;; for it, the binary. Such tests never run automatically, only when named (owner, 2026-10-23;
 ;; root's ruling 2026-10-22: by the reach walk, never a hand tag). Provenance: step E's run 4,
 ;; where a turn's tests booted roots and spun for minutes in an agent's fork; process tests
 ;; were 782 s of 917 s of recorded test time (pure-tests, 2026-10-23). A var missing here makes
 ;; its tests run automatically (slower, never wrong).
 :seon.config.source/process-vars #{seon.agent-test/in-agent clojure.java.shell/sh seon.lib/fork! seon.test-store/binary-sh}
 ;; Unit: vars. The vars through which a test reaches the built binary (target/seon): a test whose
 ;; reach references one runs it, so bin/test builds it first; a process test that only shells
 ;; out (git) does not. Provenance: root's task seon.check/a-run-builds-the-binary-only-when-
 ;; something-reaches-it (2026-10-23): bin/test built it (46 s) whenever anything ran. A var
 ;; missing here is a missed build (a test fails on a stale binary), so it names the one way a
 ;; test starts the binary: seon.test-store/binary-sh.
 :seon.config.source/binary-vars #{seon.test-store/binary-sh}
 ;; Unit: branch. Merges are committed here, never on main and never pushed (owner).
 :seon.config.source/branch "jolt/seon-merges"
 ;; Unit: a git identity. Seon authors and commits its merges (the message names the merged
 ;; tasks and runs). Stated here because a root's HOME is its checkout's target/home, which
 ;; holds no git identity (bin/check loop, 2026-10-21: "Author identity unknown").
 :seon.config.source/committer-name "Seon"
 :seon.config.source/committer-email "seon@localhost"
 ;; Unit: path. Where each generation is built (seon.fx/build, docs/source.md#the-next-generation):
 ;; <dir>/<commit>, a worktree of the merges commit (bin/worktree --at) with its own target/,
 ;; whose target/seon bin/root next starts. Kept with the run (a generation is a record of what
 ;; ran); one worktree and one ~200 MB binary per merges commit built.
 :seon.config.source/generations-dir #join [#ref [:seon.config.root/runs-dir] "/generations"]
 ;; Unit: path. Where a merge's deferred process tests run (seon.fx/run-tests, root's ruling
 ;; 2026-10-22): <dir>/<sha>-<uuid>, a worktree of the merges head with the candidate's files,
 ;; removed after its run; its log stays beside it (<dir>/<sha>-<uuid>.tests.log).
 :seon.config.source/tests-dir #join [#ref [:seon.config.root/runs-dir] "/merge-tests"]
 ;; Unit: path. Test results by reach digest (task seon.program/tests-run-by-reach; owner
 ;; 2026-10-21: "incremental tests even across worktrees based on test digest"): one directory
 ;; in the main tree, shared by every checkout and worktree, one <digest>.edn per result,
 ;; written temp-then-rename. A digest covers everything its result depends on, so a result
 ;; from any checkout is valid in every other. A checkout's store (runs/store) is its own, so
 ;; the store cannot hold them. bin/env sets SEON_TEST_RESULTS to the main tree's test-results/
 ;; (found from git's common dir, as the compile caches are); unset, the checkout's own.
 :seon.config.source/test-results-dir #or [#env SEON_TEST_RESULTS #seon/checkout "test-results"]
 ;; Unit: processes. A proof run's selected tests (bin/prove, bin/test) are dealt, a namespace (with
 ;; its fixtures) at a time, into this many shards, each a runner process of its own that bin/test
 ;; starts at once (test-runner/shards), its greens stored by digest when it has run. Provenance:
 ;; worktree session PROVE 2026-10-22 on the 16 GB M1 target (AGENTS section 8): 4 keeps the machine
 ;; responsive beside a standing root and other worktree sessions (measured in docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches).
 ;; At the bound: a run never has more shards; fewer when fewer namespaces are selected. bin/check
 ;; runs this many steps at once too, and mg-check in this many shards (pure-tests 2026-10-23).
 :seon.config.proofs/jobs 4
 ;; Unit: a salt (integer), 0 normally. Salt of every generative check's seed (seon.proofs/seed): a
 ;; seed is derived from what its check reaches (a test's reach digest, a contract's var and the
 ;; binary's tree), so the same digest draws the same cases and a result is a function of its
 ;; digest, while a code change draws new ones. Change it to re-roll every seed once. Provenance:
 ;; worktree session pure-tests 2026-10-23 (mg/check drew from the wall clock; owner via root:
 ;; derive each seed from its digest, not one constant). At the bound: none.
 :seon.config.proofs/seed-salt 0
 ;; Unit: the size n of a cost proof's input (seon.cost/sizes). A proof measures its sizes up to
 ;; this, and at least its two smallest (a growth fit needs two). Provenance: root, 2026-10-23
 ;; (notes/test-time-2026-10-23.md: building inputs at 10k took 10-19 s per proof in every run).
 ;; bin/test --all sets SEON_COST_MAX_N=100000, so the full run measures each proof as written.
 ;; At the bound: the proof measures no size past this but its two smallest.
 :seon.config.proofs/cost-max-n #long #or [#env SEON_COST_MAX_N 1000]
 ;; Unit: seeds (a count). How many seeds a randomized property test draws in this run, each
 ;; from its own test-seed (seon.proofs/test-seed) and the next ones after it. Provenance: root,
 ;; 2026-10-23 (notes/test-time-2026-10-23.md: r3.inc-test's 5 seeds x 150 steps took 47 s, the
 ;; automatic run's critical path). bin/test --all sets SEON_SEEDS=5, so the full run draws as
 ;; many as before. At the bound: the test draws this many and no more.
 :seon.config.proofs/seeds #long #or [#env SEON_SEEDS 1]

 ;; ==== effects and the model ==================================================================

 ;; Unit: requests one effect owner holds queued (its in-port and its priority queue). Each
 ;; agent has at most one synchronous effect in flight, so beyond the live agents only async
 ;; sends queue. At the bound agent procs park (back-pressure). Session FX1.
 :seon.config.fx/buf 1024

 ;; Who answers agents' turns. With no profile, none: the first turn answers the typed
 ;; :seon.model/no-provider and the run ends on it (root's ruling 2026-10-22: production
 ;; ran the scripted test fixture as if it were a model). SEON_PROFILE=deepseek (or
 ;; SEON_AI_PROVIDER=openai-compat) uses the endpoint below; SEON_PROFILE=scripted, the
 ;; scripted provider (a test fixture) bin/check and bin/test run under, no paid call: it needs
 ;; that profile, which states its script (SEON_AI_PROVIDER=scripted alone leaves none, and boot
 ;; refuses the provider's contract). There is no default model (docs/model.md#no-default-model);
 ;; check the context before any paid call.
 :seon.config.ai/provider #keyword #or [#env SEON_AI_PROVIDER #profile {:default "none" :scripted "scripted" :deepseek "openai-compat"}]
 ;; The scripted provider's reply function (a test fixture): replies by task.
 :seon.config.ai/script #profile {:scripted seon.fixtures/reply}
 ;; Unit: the provider's own model name. Provenance: v1 owner ruling 2026-09-10, deepseek-flash
 ;; is the default for every agent.
 :seon.config.ai/model #or [#env SEON_AI_MODEL "deepseek-flash"]
 ;; Unit: completion tokens, reasoning included. Provenance: seon.model's default-max-tokens
 ;; (2,048) before this file. At the bound the reply is cut (:truncated?), or
 ;; the reasoning ate the reply (:seon.model/reasoning-ate-reply). v1 ships 65,536 for Flash
 ;; with thinking on (its 4,096 and 8,192 starved reasoning). Ported 2026-10-21 after the first
 ;; DeepSeek agent on Seon's own code: two refinement runs answered empty, reasoning took all
 ;; 2,048. 2026-10-22, step E (root's research, notes/agent-context-research-2026-10-22.md §3.3):
 ;; 8,192 while thinking is off (the default below). The largest ordinary reply measured ~5k;
 ;; loop replies ran for minutes (168 of 181 model-minutes, 12.4M tokens, AUDIT-agents §5.2),
 ;; and a task whose every reply runs to 65,536 costs ~$5.8 (one such reply ~$0.04). A task or
 ;; model that turns thinking on raises it through the resolver (v1's 65,536 for that case).
 :seon.config.ai/max-tokens #long #or [#env SEON_AI_MAX_TOKENS 8192]
 ;; DeepSeek's thinking mode (:enabled | :disabled). Off (owner, 2026-10-21: "if we can't have
 ;; intuitive function calling based on the context our context is bad and instructions need
 ;; help"): a task whose next step needs long reasoning is a context or instruction defect to
 ;; fix, not a cost to pay every turn. A model or task can turn it on through the resolver
 ;; (task -> model -> provider -> config); effort is :seon.config.ai/reasoning-effort.
 :seon.config.ai/thinking :disabled
 ;; Unit: model calls in flight at once, cluster-wide: our admission cap, the :model owner's
 ;; workers (session FX1). At the bound turns queue in the owner, by priority. Our choice, small:
 ;; eight is twice the runs running at once (:seon.config.agent/max-running 4), so a
 ;; merge's or a judge's call never waits behind the work, while a runaway loop spends at most
 ;; eight calls' tokens at a time. The provider's own ceiling, per account across all keys
 ;; (api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/rate_limit, read 2026-10-21): 2,500 concurrent for
 ;; deepseek-flash, 500 for deepseek-v4-pro; far above this, so the cap is ours, not theirs.
 :seon.config.ai/max-in-flight 8

 ;; ---- the model manifest (v1's config/default.edn initialization rows, ported for the models
 ;; we use). A setting resolves task -> model -> its provider -> this file's top-level key
 ;; (seon.config/resolve, the one way code reads a setting); root writes each provider and model
 ;; as an entity on main (platform records). A model or provider states a setting by its
 ;; :seon.config.* key to override the top-level one for itself.
 :seon.config.ai/providers
 {"deepseek"
  {;; Unit: URL; /chat/completions is appended (v1's :seon.config.ai/endpoint named the whole URL).
   :seon.config.ai/base-url #or [#env SEON_AI_BASE_URL "https://api.deepseek.com"]
   ;; Unit: the NAME of an environment variable. Never the credential: root's :model owner reads
   ;; the key at the call, it never becomes a datom, and every agent unsets this variable at its
   ;; birth (the key is root's alone).
   :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable #or [#env SEON_AI_API_KEY_ENV "DEEPSEEK_API_KEY"]
   ;; the request field the completion budget is sent in (v1)
   :seon.ai.model/output-token-wire-key "max_tokens"}}
 ;; Prices: USD per million tokens, off-peak; peak (01:00-04:00 and 06:00-10:00 UTC, weekdays,
 ;; not Chinese public holidays) is twice. Read 2026-10-21 from
 ;; api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing. Concurrency ceilings: the provider's, per account
 ;; across all keys, read 2026-10-21 from api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/rate_limit; our own
 ;; cap is :seon.config.ai/max-in-flight.
 :seon.config.ai/models
 {"deepseek-flash"
  {:seon.ai.model/provider "deepseek"
   ;; DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017; the legacy names deepseek-v4-flash(-vision-exp) route here
   :seon.ai.model/version "DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017"
   :seon.ai.model/context-window-tokens 1048576
   :seon.ai.model/max-output-tokens 393216
   :seon.ai.model/input-usd-per-mtok 0.15
   :seon.ai.model/cached-input-usd-per-mtok 0.003
   :seon.ai.model/output-usd-per-mtok 0.6
   :seon.ai.model/thinking-modes #{:enabled :disabled}
   :seon.ai.model/provider-max-in-flight 2500
   :seon.ai.model/source "api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing and /rate_limit, read 2026-10-21"}
  "deepseek-v4-pro"
  {:seon.ai.model/provider "deepseek"
   :seon.ai.model/version "DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813"
   :seon.ai.model/context-window-tokens 1048576
   :seon.ai.model/max-output-tokens 393216
   :seon.ai.model/input-usd-per-mtok 0.66
   :seon.ai.model/cached-input-usd-per-mtok 0.022
   :seon.ai.model/output-usd-per-mtok 1.98
   :seon.ai.model/thinking-modes #{:enabled :disabled}
   :seon.ai.model/provider-max-in-flight 500
   :seon.ai.model/source "api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing and /rate_limit, read 2026-10-21"}}
 ;; Unit: tokens per second the provider admits, cluster-wide: the :model owner's token bucket
 ;; (session R3; AIMD on a 429). 0: no bucket.
 :seon.config.ai/rate 0
 ;; Unit: characters per token, the one estimate the printer and the context size with.
 ;; Provenance: v1's :seon.config.ai/chars-per-token-prior, 17 DeepSeek prompt samples
 ;; (2026-08-08/10) fit 3.21-3.23.
 :seon.config.ai/chars-per-token-prior 3.2

 ;; ==== the printer (seon.print: every value a person or a model reads) ========================

 ;; Unit: tokens a reply's value may spend. Provenance: v1's
 ;; :seon.config.render.agent/token-budget (owner 2026-10-12: print more, so help and the
 ;; current-state block show whole). Past it the value is cut, every cut carrying the form
 ;; that reads its rest.
 :seon.config.render.agent/token-budget 6144
 ;; Unit: nested levels shown (v1's :seon.config.render.agent/max-depth).
 :seon.config.render.agent/max-depth 8
 ;; Unit: characters. A value whose laid-out text is at most this wide is never cut: its cut
 ;; would say less than the value (v1).
 :seon.config.render.agent/whole-width 24
 ;; Unit: characters. A keyword or symbol is one name, whole up to this length (v1).
 :seon.config.render.agent/max-name 256
 ;; Unit: decimal digits. A BigInt, Ratio or BigDecimal past this prints as its digit count (v1).
 :seon.config.render.agent/max-number-digits 32
 ;; Unit: keys. A keyword-keyed map or set of at most this many shows identity attributes and
 ;; short members first, then alphabetically; a larger one its own seq order (v1).
 :seon.config.render.agent/sort-bound 64
 ;; Unit: characters per printed line (session P1's printer, matching v1's corpus output).
 :seon.config.render/width 72
 ;; Unit: tokens for a value shown inside another: a refused argument, a test's expected and
 ;; actual, ex-data (session P1).
 :seon.config.render/part-budget 256
 ;; Unit: tokens for a form echoed back (session P1).
 :seon.config.render/form-budget 600
 ;; Unit: tokens for the context block the model reads (the task, its proofs, what it cites),
 ;; breadth first so every section shows before any one deepens (session P1). The evaluations and
 ;; the history have their own budgets below; none is carved out of another.
 :seon.config.render/context-budget 16000
 ;; Unit: tokens one turn's evaluations show the model, each an equal share, every cut naming
 ;; the form that reads its rest. Provenance: seon.context's literal last-evals-budget (6000),
 ;; 2026-10-21 it was raised to 24000 (a live agent reading four Datahike functions got ~1500
 ;; tokens each and re-read them); with 256000 of history that sent ~300k tokens a turn (an
 ;; run of 13 turns spent 4.1M), so 8000: a long read is cut with the form that reads its rest.
 :seon.config.render/evals-budget 8000
 ;; Unit: tokens of earlier turns (each the model's reply and what its forms evaluated to) the
 ;; model reads before the current state, whole turns, the newest kept first; the task's first
 ;; context always stays. 0 shows only the last turn. Provenance (owner, 2026-10-21, after a live
 ;; agent re-read the same Datahike source every other turn because each context showed only
 ;; the previous turn): a REPL keeps its scrollback. 256000 (2026-10-21) resent ~300k tokens a
 ;; turn (owner: "wtf"); 32000 keeps the last several whole turns (a turn is a reply of ~1-3k
 ;; plus its evaluations up to the evals budget), so a turn sends ~20-55k with the block.
 :seon.config.render/history-budget 32000
 ;; Unit: tokens of one cited var's source in the context (cited in three backticks). Past it
 ;; the var shows at doc depth with how many tokens were left out and the form that reads them,
 ;; (clojure.repl/source ns/f). Provenance: v1's must-reads per-function cap, reference-tokens
 ;; 3000 (audit notes/AUDIT-v1-to-next-2026-10-22.md 2.3).
 :seon.config.render/reference-tokens 3000
 ;; Unit: vars. The helpers the context names that the plan does not (:found): program functions
 ;; the plan's cited vars and the proofs call. 0 shows none. Provenance: R4's k = 5
 ;; (notes/option3-R4-learning.md 5: 14 of 16 needed helpers in the top 5).
 :seon.config.render/found 5
 ;; Unit: tokens (seon.print.select/estimate). The run's map of the namespaces its proofs and
 ;; cited vars live in (seon.context/ns-map): each namespace's functions, most referenced first,
 ;; within an equal share. Provenance: root's research (2026-10-22, lever 1), aider's default
 ;; --map-tokens 1k. At the bound a namespace's rest is (clojure.repl/dir ns).
 :seon.config.render/map-tokens 1000
 ;; Unit: items. A list shown in part (seon.print/bounded) shows this many, then `+N more:
 ;; where` (docs/render.md#bounded-lists). Provenance: the literals each list carried before
 ;; (render.ui and render.ai, 2026-10-22), kept as they were.
 ;; rows: running and newest runs, root's context's newest events.
 :seon.config.render.list/rows 8
 ;; newest: a tile's or card's newest (root's defects on the page, a card's evaluations).
 :seon.config.render.list/newest 6
 ;; brief: a secondary list in root's context (ended runs, costliest functions).
 :seon.config.render.list/brief 5
 ;; top: root's context's defects, the evaluations its costliest functions come from, a
 ;; card's top functions.
 :seon.config.render.list/top 3
 ;; Unit: bytes. An evaluation that allocated more is flagged on its card and row
 ;; (seon.render.ui/alloc-span). Provenance: render.ui's BIG-ALLOC literal (10 MiB).
 :seon.config.render/big-alloc 10485760
 ;; Unit: tokens a rendered value spends when its env names no budget (seon.render/print-html and print-ai,
 ;; seon.render/ai, an agent's history row). Provenance: the literal 600 render.clj and
 ;; render.ui carried (2026-10-22), kept as it was.
 :seon.config.render/value-budget 600
 ;; Unit: tokens of root's context, the system's AI face (seon.render.ai/system); past it the
 ;; later sections are named, not shown. Provenance: render.ai's and the render proc's 700.
 :seon.config.render/root-context-budget 700
 ;; Unit: tokens each evaluation's value shows in an agent's history on its page (the render
 ;; proc renders each row once). Provenance: the render proc's literal 400.
 :seon.config.render/history-row-budget 400
 ;; Unit: tokens each part of a turn on its agent's page shows (its reply, reasoning, each eval
 ;; call's code and answer, the context it read): past it the cut links the turn's whole request
 ;; (/turn?view=&id=). Provenance: the context's two messages are each under 16,000
 ;; tokens (seon.context/messages); measured 2026-10-22, a turn's run sections are 1.6-1.9k
 ;; and its turn sections under 0.1k before a proof runs, so 4,000 shows most turns whole.
 :seon.config.render/turn-text-budget 4000
 ;; Unit: tokens of a message or reason shown in one line (root's context, a tile's or card's
 ;; line, a declared AI render's line); past it the line's cut names the form that reads the
 ;; rest. Provenance: the literals 40-60 render.ui and render.ai carried (2026-10-22), as one.
 :seon.config.render/line-budget 50
 ;; Unit: tokens of a table cell or a stream row's field (a form, a value, a reason); past it the
 ;; cut says what it omitted and links the page that shows it whole. Provenance: the literals
 ;; 12-30 render.ui carried (2026-10-22), as one.
 :seon.config.render/cell-budget 25
 ;; Unit: events the dashboard's stream keeps (the render proc), newest first; its header says
 ;; how many older ones it did not keep. Provenance: the render proc's EVENT-CAP 60.
 :seon.config.render.list/stream 60
 ;; Unit: points a sparkline keeps, the newest changes. Provenance: the render proc's SERIES-CAP 60.
 :seon.config.render.list/series 60
 ;; Unit: ended agents the dashboard's grid keeps beside every live one, the newest ended first;
 ;; an agent's end pushes the oldest kept one off, and the tile counts those off it. Provenance:
 ;; replaces seon.render.proc's RETIRE-MS (90 s after an end), a clock deciding what the grid shows.
 :seon.config.render.list/ended 10
 ;; Unit: evaluations an agent's page shows, newest first (seon.print/bounded names the rest
 ;; on its branch). Provenance: the render proc's HISTORY-CAP 100.
 :seon.config.render.list/history 100

 ;; ==== the web, instrumentation, the native probe ==============================================

 ;; Unit: TCP port on 127.0.0.1 (session W2). 0 (the shipped value): no dashboard. A checkout's
 ;; own config/local.edn names its port (bin/ports writes it: the main tree 7777, worktree k
 ;; 7901+10k, from one registry), so no two checkouts share one (owner, 2026-10-21). bin/check
 ;; and bin/test set SEON_WEB_PORT=0, which local.edn honours, so check steps listen on nothing.
 ;; A taken port leaves the web proc {:listening false} on the health line and boot goes on.
 :seon.config.web/port #long #or [#env SEON_WEB_PORT 0]
 ;; Unit: TCP port on 127.0.0.1: root's server (seon.server), which bin/mcp and outside
 ;; agents reach. Three values: >0 that port; 0 (the shipped value) none, `seon root` refuses to
 ;; serve; -1 a free port the kernel assigns. The port bound is named on the root's ROOT LISTENING
 ;; line and recorded in runs/root.port (a test's root: seon.test-store/start-root!,
 ;; never a port picked and released beforehand, which another process can take). config/local.edn names
 ;; the checkout's own (main 7888, worktree k 7900+10k).
 ;; bin/root PORT WEB, bin/mcp PORT and the mcp check set SEON_SERVER_PORT, which local.edn
 ;; honours (until a built binary's top-level code sees its argv, so --set can say it).
 :seon.config.root/server-port #long #or [#env SEON_SERVER_PORT 0]
 ;; app.css and datastar.js, read per request so a style edit shows on reload.
 :seon.config.web/public-dir #seon/checkout "resources/public"
 ;; Unit: +hhmm. Jolt has no time zones: clocks on the page add this offset (`date +%z`).
 ;; Unset: UTC, which the page's clocks then show.
 :seon.config.web/utc-offset #or [#env SEON_UTC_OFFSET "+0000"]

 ;; Contracts and their recording (session G1): :on, :contracts (wrappers, no recording
 ;; transaction: the two measured apart), :off (no wrapper, no recording: the same binary
 ;; measured with instrumentation off; test/r3's benches).
 :seon.config.contract/instrument #keyword #or [#env SEON_INSTRUMENT "on"]

 ;; native/probe.c's library (bin/build native): Mach memory probes and qfork. T5 points it at
 ;; next's build from a snapshot that has none.
 :seon.config.native/probe-lib #or [#env SEON_PROBE_LIB #seon/checkout "target/lib/libprobe.dylib"]}
